Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  

- Vmag (apparent magnitude) indicates how bright a star appears from Earth; giants can be bright or dim depending on distance, so Vmag alone is not a strong indicator.  
- Plx (parallax) measures distance; giants tend to be more luminous but can be farther away, so smaller Plx might hint at giants but not conclusively.  
- e_Plx (parallax error) affects distance accuracy; large errors reduce reliability of distance-based features.  
- B-V (color index) relates to star temperature and spectral type; giants often have higher B-V (redder), but overlap exists.  
- SpType (spectral type) directly encodes luminosity class (V = main sequence, III = giant, II = bright giant, IV = subgiant). This is the strongest indicator.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) is intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.  

Step 2. Based on Step 1 and the examples, infer 10 different conditions per answer class:

10 different conditions for class "0" (Not Giant):  
- SpType is in [types containing "V"] (e.g., F8V, F5V, A8V, B9V)  
- SpType is in [types containing "IV"] but not "III" or "II" (e.g., F7/F8IV)  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) >= 17  
- B-V <= 0.6  
- Plx >= 6  
- Vmag >= 6 and Vmag <= 10  
- e_Plx <= 1.5  
- SpType does not contain "III" or "II" or "III/IV" or "II/III"  
- Amag is within range of [17, 21]  
- B-V is within range of [0.2, 0.6]  

10 different conditions for class "1" (Giant):  
- SpType is in [types containing "III"] or [types containing "II"] or [types containing "III/IV"] or [types containing "II/III"] (e.g., K2III, G8III, K3II/III, G8III/IV)  
- Amag <= 16  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx <= 5  
- Vmag >= 5 and Vmag <= 8  
- e_Plx <= 1.2  
- SpType contains "III" and B-V >= 0.9  
- Amag is within range of [14, 16.5]  
- B-V is within range of [0.9, 1.6]  
- Plx is within range of [1.5, 5]  

These conditions align well with the examples and astrophysical knowledge about giants vs dwarfs.

---DIVIDER---

Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs; alone it is less decisive without distance.  
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants tend to be luminous and thus visible from farther away, often having smaller parallax if faint.  
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude; less directly related to giant classification.  
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature; giants often have higher B-V (redder), but spectral type is more definitive.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class (e.g., III, II) in spectral type directly indicates giant (III), bright giant (II), subgiant (IV), or main sequence (V).  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have brighter absolute magnitudes (lower numerical values) compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.  

Step 2. Based on the above and examples, here are conditions for each answer class:  

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, A8V, B9V, F5V, F8V, F7/F8IV]  
- Amag >= 17  
- B-V <= 0.6  
- Plx >= 6  
- Vmag >= 7.5  
- e_Plx <= 1.1  
- SpType ends with "V" (main sequence)  
- Amag is within range [17, 21]  
- B-V is within range [0.2, 0.6]  
- Plx is within range [6, 30]  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [M1III, G6III, G8III, K2III, K3II/III, K0III, K1III, G8III/IV, K2III]  
- Amag <= 16  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx <= 5  
- Vmag <= 7.7  
- e_Plx >= 0.7  
- SpType contains "III" or "II" or "III/IV" (indicating giant or bright giant)  
- Amag is within range [14, 16]  
- B-V is within range [0.9, 1.6]  
- Plx is within range [1.9, 5.5]

