Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs, but alone it is not a strong indicator of giant status because it depends on distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants tend to be more luminous and visible at larger distances (smaller parallax), but this alone is not definitive.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; high error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude estimates.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants tend to have higher B-V (redder), but some overlap exists.
- SpType (spectral type): This is a strong indicator; luminosity classes III, II, or IV (giants, bright giants, subgiants) indicate giant stars, while V indicates main sequence (dwarf) stars.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Intrinsic brightness; giants are generally more luminous (lower or more negative absolute magnitude) than dwarfs.

