Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): A giant star can be bright or faint depending on distance, so Vmag alone is not a strong indicator.  
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means the star is farther away. Giants tend to be luminous and visible at larger distances, so giants might have smaller parallaxes on average.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty, not directly related to star type.  
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and color. Giants tend to be cooler (redder) and have higher B-V values.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III for giants) directly indicates if the star is a giant.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.  

